WEBVTT

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:30.640
Short and sweet number seven. We discussed how the Rishonim have a machlokas, whether or not the Isser of Hanah, the prohibition of benefiting from Stam Yeinam, still applies nowadays. We mentioned that Rashi and the Geonim say that it is permissible to benefit from Stam Yeinam nowadays because non-Jews generally do not libate anymore. They still hold that. Drinking the wine is a problem, but benefiting is permitted. The Rush held that if it's a situation

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.100
The Rosh Hashanah is a situation where you had wine, which was owned by a Jew, and it was just touched by a non-Jew.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.980
That's a scenario, the Rosh Hashanah said, we can allow benefit.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.760
It's only where the, it's a case of Stam Yeinam, where the non-Jew owned the wine himself.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.500
That's where the Rosh Hashanah said, we still hold that benefit is prohibited.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:54.960
The Rambam held that it doesn't matter whether it was touched by a non-Jew or owned by a non-Jew, benefiting from it is prohibited.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:04.360
So what comes out, Halach HaMaisah, is that when it comes to drinking wine which was touched or owned by a non-Jew, that is forbidden even b'zban haZeh, there is no machlokas.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:14.740
However, when it comes to benefiting from Stam Yeinam, which is referring to wine which was either owned by a non-Jew or wine which was touched by a non-Jew, we had a machlokas between the Shulchan Aruch and the Rama.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:25.000
The Shulchan Aruch holds, and the Rama holds even L'Chachila as well, that wine which was either owned by a non-Jew or touched by a non-Jew, it is forbidden to derive any sort of benefit from.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:38.060
However, in a situation which was Bidiyavid, a significant loss, the Rama did hold that either case, whether the wine was owned by a non-Jew or wine which was touched by a non-Jew, would be permitted to benefit from.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:52.940
He does not allow drinking, as we mentioned, that's an issue of intermarriage, but because the libation is not so much a problem anymore, Jews don't libate, sorry, non-Jews do not libate, we do not forbid benefiting from the wine.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:02:04.360
Now even according to the Rama, who permits benefiting from Stam Yeinam nowadays, he still strongly discourages a person from making a living by selling Stam Yeinam, this is more of a Shasad Chak Heter.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.520
Okay, when it came to re-gifting a bottle of Stam Yeinam, the Shulchan Aruch clearly holds that that would be a problem.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:15.600
The Rama does give us a little bit more leniency, however, it would only be in a situation of a great loss,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.320
and also we mentioned that it cannot be done in the context of a non-Jewish holiday.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:29.160
If it's an inexpensive bottle of wine and there's really no great loss, so even an Ashkenazi should not re-gift the bottle and should not derive any benefit.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:40.080
The same makhlukahs would then play out in a situation where there is a group gift being given to an employer, and the bottle of wine is going to be the gift, and the bottle of wine is Stam Yeinam.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:45.080
So again, the Rav Moshe would say it would be forbidden to chip into this to benefit from Stam Yeinam.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:53.540
However, the Rama would allow it if it's a situation of by refraining from chipping into the gift would translate into a significant loss.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:03:15.060
We discussed the question of purchasing kosher wine from a store owned by non-Jews, so that is totally permitted because in this scenario, the wine was owned by Jews originally, and it was produced in the ownership of a Jew, and therefore it's not considered Stam Yeinam, as long as the bottle is sealed, it would be considered kosher.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:22.600
The question really came up is purchasing wine from a Jewish store, a Jewish-owned store, which also sells Stam Yeinam.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.620
In this scenario, so the Jewish owner is not allowed to sell Stam Yeinam, he's not allowed to get benefit,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.960
and therefore if he sold a bottle of Stam Yeinam to someone, the money which he received for it is Aser Bahana,

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.520
he's not allowed to get Hana from it, and neither is anyone else.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.700
If in this case, if you would walk into the store and purchase a kosher bottle of wine,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.240
and use cash to purchase the bottle, then there could be a problem if you receive change,

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:51.620
Because the change that you're receiving, the cash change that you're receiving, could be money which was used in the transaction of Stam Yeinam, which would be Aser Behanah.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:58.220
The only solution we, the two solutions we gave was either use exact change or to buy with a credit card.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:10.040
Finally, we did mention that although this prohibition of Stam Yeinam is only rabbinic, only being very heavily in quotations, but it still is a very, very severe, it is a very severe prohibition.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:19.380
And although Chazal revealed one of the reasons for this gzera, there are many more unknown reasons which apply, even if the original reasons don't apply anymore, and therefore we are very machmer when it comes to Stam Yeinam.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.100
Some people go so far to consider wine which was only looked at by a non-Jew as Stam Yeinam.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.060
There are those who say that that's only in a situation where the wine is being used for a mitzvah.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.600
There are those who say it doesn't apply when the wine is in a bottle, or when the wine is sealed.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:42.420
Rav Yashiv Paschim, L'maisah, L'mikra Adin, according to the letter of the law, we do not forbid wine which is only looked at by a non-Jew, even to use for a mitzvah, however there are those who are machmer as a hider.
